{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"FSI Spotlight: Amazon ECR | AWS for Industries","context":"### Lifecycle Policies\n\nAmazon ECR provides lifecycle policies to help you manage the lifecycle of images in your repositories. While this is more about operational efficiency, it can also be a part of a security strategy by ensuring the automated removal of old and potentially insecure images","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/industries/fsi-spotlight-amazon-ecr/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Lifecycle policy properties in Amazon ECR","context":"## Rule priority\n\n`rulePriority`\n: Type: integer\n\n Required: yes\n\n Sets the order in which rules are applied, lowest to highest. A\n lifecycle policy rule with a priority of `1` is applied\n first, a rule with priority of `2` is next, and so on. When\n you add rules to a lifecycle policy, you must give them each a unique\n value for `rulePriority`. Values don't need to be sequential\n across rules in a policy. A rule with a `tagStatus` value of\n `any` must have the highest value for\n `rulePriority` and be evaluated last","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/lifecycle_policy_parameters.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Examples of lifecycle policies in Amazon ECR","context":"### Example A\n\nRepository contents:\n\n* Image A, Taglist: [\"beta-1\", \"prod-1\"], Pushed: 10 days ago\n* Image B, Taglist: [\"beta-2\", \"prod-2\"], Pushed: 9 days ago\n* Image C, Taglist: [\"beta-3\"], Pushed: 8 days ago\n\nLifecycle policy text:\n\n```\n{\n \"rules\": [\n {\n \"rulePriority\": 1,\n \"description\": \"Rule 1\",\n \"selection\": {\n \"tagStatus\": \"tagged\",\n \"tagPatternList\": [\"prod*\"],\n \"countType\": \"imageCountMoreThan\",\n \"countNumber\": 1\n },\n \"action\": {\n \"type\": \"expire\"\n }\n },\n {\n \"rulePriority\": 2,\n \"description\": \"Rule 2\",\n \"selection\": {\n \"tagStatus\": \"tagged\",\n \"tagPatternList\": [\"beta*\"],\n \"countType\": \"imageCountMoreThan\",\n \"countNumber\": 1\n },\n \"action\": {\n \"type\": \"expire\"\n }\n }\n ]\n}\n```\n\nThe logic of this lifecycle policy would be:\n\n* Rule 1 identifies images tagged with prefix `prod`. It\n should mark images, starting with the oldest, until there is one or\n fewer images remaining that match. It marks Image A for\n expiration.\n* Rule 2 identifies images tagged with prefix `beta`. It\n should mark images, starting with the oldest, until there is one or\n fewer images remaining that match. It marks both Image A and Image B for\n expiration. However, Image A has already been seen by Rule 1 and if\n Image B were expired it would violate Rule 1 and thus is skipped.\n* Result: Image A is expired","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/lifecycle_policy_examples.html"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon ECR introduces archive storage class for rarely accessed container images - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) has introduced a new archive storage class to help reduce storage costs for infrequently accessed container images. This new feature allows users to meet compliance and retention requirements while optimizing storage expenses. ECR's lifecycle policies now support archiving images based on last pull time, enabling automatic archiving based on usage patterns. Users can also archive images individually using the ECR Console or API. Archived images do not count towards the image per repository limit and are inaccessible for pulls but can be easily restored within 20 minutes via the ECR Console, CLI, or API. All archival and restore operations are logged through CloudTrail for auditability. The new ECR archive storage class is available in all AWS Commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For pricing details, visit the AWS pricing page. To learn more, consult the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-ecr-archive-storage-class-container-images/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-resilience-lifecycle","skill_description":"Guides the end-to-end AWS resilience lifecycle integrating Resilience Hub v2, Fault Injection Service, and Application Recovery Controller. Covers the Define → Test → Operate workflow: from policy creation through failure mode assessment, to FIS experiment validation, to ARC operational controls. Applicable when the user wants a complete resilience strategy, needs to connect findings to experiments to controls, or is planning a resilience program. Also applicable for the meta question of whether marking NGRH findings as resolved is enough, whether they are \"done\" after resolving findings, or how to validate findings before resolving them. Not applicable for resolving or remediating a specific individual finding (see resilience-hub-failure-mode-assessment), or when a single service is explicitly named (e.g. \"what FIS experiment should I run\").\n\n\nServices: resiliencehub, fis, route53-recovery-control-config, arc-zonal-shift\nTasks: configure, test, audit\nPersona: architect, sre, devops\nWorkload: resilience\n*","skill_name":"aws-resilience-lifecycle"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"EC2 Image Builder enhances lifecycle policies with wildcard support and simplified IAM - AWS","context":"EC2 Image Builder, a service offered by AWS for automating the creation, distribution, and management of customized Amazon Machine Images, has introduced two new features to simplify image management. Firstly, wildcard patterns are now supported in lifecycle policies, allowing users to manage images from multiple recipes within a single policy. This feature automatically applies lifecycle policies to all matching recipes, including new ones created in the future. Secondly, IAM role creation for lifecycle management has been simplified, with the console now automatically populating the required default permissions when creating a new role. Previously, users had to create separate lifecycle policies for each new recipe or manually configure permissions, making it difficult to manage image lifecycles at scale. These enhancements reduce setup time and potential configuration errors, enabling users to manage their image lifecycle more efficiently. Lifecycle Policies are available in all commercial AWS regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/ec2-image-builder-lifecycle-enhancements/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"processing-s3-uploads-with-step-functions","skill_description":"Deploy an event-driven workflow that routes S3 uploads to either Lambda or Fargate via Step Functions based on file size. Uses EventBridge to trigger a Step Functions state machine when objects are uploaded to S3. Small files are processed by Lambda, large files by a Fargate task. Includes VPC, ECR repository, ECS cluster, and scoped IAM roles. Trigger keywords: Step Functions, Fargate, Lambda, S3 event, EventBridge, ECS, ECR, file processing, workflow orchestration, serverless.\n\n\nServices: step-functions, lambda, s3, eventbridge, ecs, ecr, fargate\nTasks: deploy, configure\nPersona: backend-developer\nWorkload: serverless, containers\n*","skill_name":"processing-s3-uploads-with-step-functions"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"AWS Config now supports 191 additional managed rules - AWS","context":"AWS Config has added support for 191 new managed rules across various services including Amazon Bedrock, Amazon SageMaker, Amazon ECS, Amazon EKS, Amazon RDS, Amazon Redshift, Amazon S3, and Amazon CloudTrail. These rules cover encryption, logging, public access, network security, data protection, and operational best practices. Users can deploy these rules individually or as part of a conformance pack. The new rules include ACM_CERTIFICATE_RSA_CHECK for AWS Certificate Manager, API_GWV2_ACCESS_LOGS_ENABLED for Amazon API Gateway, APPSYNC_AUTHORIZATION_CHECK and APPSYNC_LOGGING_ENABLED for AWS AppSync, and many more for other services. The expansion increases built-in governance coverage across AI workloads and core cloud infrastructure","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-config-additional-managed-rules/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verifying ECR lifecycle policy rule evaluation order semantics in AWS docs"}
